-- title:
--   Lemma 6.5 — ε-optimal stationary strategies in $D(m\ell_{\mathrm{str}},B_{\mathrm{str}})$
-- statement:
--   Consider a game $G$ with a reward function $r$ (values in $[0,1]$) and a succinct discount factor $\lambda=1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}}$. Let $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}$, $n=|S|$, $m=\max(|A|,|B|)$, and
--   $$\ell_{\mathrm{str}}=B_\varepsilon+\lceil\log(n+2)\rceil+\lceil\log m\rceil+5,\qquad B_{\mathrm{str}}=B_\lambda+B_\varepsilon+\lceil\log m\rceil+\ell_{\mathrm{str}}+3.$$
--   Then there exist $\varepsilon$-optimal stationary strategies $\sigma$ and $\tau$ for $\mathrm{Disc}(r,\lambda)$ with $\sigma(s),\tau(s)\in D(m\ell_{\mathrm{str}},B_{\mathrm{str}})$ for all states $s$.
--
--   These are the polynomial-size strategies of the witness.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: ⌈log x⌉ (base 2) → Nat.clog 2 x; reason: equal for every positive integer x; all logarithm arguments are positive because the state and action sets are nonempty
--   - generalization: the computational accuracy convention ε = 2^{-Bε} ∈ (0,1), Bε ≥ 1 → Bε ranges over all natural numbers, also allowing ε = 1; reason: all source instances remain included; at Bε = 0 all values lie in [0,1], so the value-error bound is automatic and pure stationary strategies have the stated power-series size and are 1-optimal
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Lemma 6.5, printed p. 20 (PDF p. 22)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_PowerSeries
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Lemma 6.5 (Polynomial-size ε-optimal strategies).** For a game `G` with reward function `r`,
succinct discount factor `λ = 1 - 2^{-2^{Bλ}}` and `ε = 2^{-Bε}`, let
`ℓ_str = Bε + ⌈log(n+2)⌉ + ⌈log m⌉ + 5` and `B_str = Bλ + Bε + ⌈log m⌉ + ℓ_str + 3`. Then there
exist `ε`-optimal stationary strategies `σ, τ` with `σ(s), τ(s) ∈ D(m ℓ_str, B_str)` for all
states. -/
theorem lemma_6_5 {n mA mB : ℕ} (G : CSG n mA mB) (r : Reward n mA mB) (hr : IsReward r)
    (Blam Bε : ℕ) :
    ∃ (σ : Stationary₁ n mA) (τ : Stationary₂ n mB),
      IsEpsOptimal₁ G (discObj r (succinct Blam)) ((1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ Bε) (σ.toStrategy mB) ∧
      IsEpsOptimal₂ G (discObj r (succinct Blam)) ((1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ Bε) (τ.toStrategy mA) ∧
      (∀ s, (σ s).1 ∈ Dset mA (max mA mB * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 2) + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) + 5))
        (Blam + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) +
          (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 2) + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) + 5) + 3)) ∧
      (∀ s, (τ s).1 ∈ Dset mB (max mA mB * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 2) + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) + 5))
        (Blam + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) +
          (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 2) + Nat.clog 2 (max mA mB) + 5) + 3)) := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
